<p>The "goods readied" range comes from our product buyer guides. For example, pulses ship from cleaned, graded stock in 7–12 days, while frozen seafood runs 15–20 days, subject to a production slot. Fresh produce also depends on the harvest window. Transit ranges by region are on <a href="/logistics-shipping/">Logistics &amp; Shipping</a>, from 2–7 days for South Asia to 25–40 days for Latin America.</p>

<h2>The first reply: what lands in your inbox</h2>
<p>Within 24 business hours you hear back with one of two things. If your requirement is specific, you get confirmation of what's available at your specification, often with packaging and container-loading details. If it isn't, you get clarifying questions: grade, size, moisture, packaging format, labelling, whatever applies to the product.</p>
<p>That second outcome isn't a delay on our side. A requirement like "good quality rice" can't be priced honestly, because the price range across grades is wide. Answering those questions in one reply, rather than one at a time, is the single easiest way to shorten a first order.</p>
<p>If a product is out of season, restricted for export at the time, or in a grade we can't currently source, the first reply says so. We'd rather tell you that on day one than after a round of samples.</p>

<h2>From price offer to proforma invoice</h2>
<p>The price offer states the Incoterm with a named port ("FOB Mundra", "CIF Jebel Ali"), the quantity it applies to, packaging, and a validity window. Commodity and freight prices move, so the offer isn't open-ended. If the window passes while you're deciding, ask for a refreshed price rather than assuming the old figure holds.</p>
<p>Once you accept, we issue a proforma invoice. It sets out the product and specification, quantity, packaging, Incoterm and named port, price, payment terms and shipment window. Read it as carefully as a contract, because in practice it is the document everything else is checked against. Your bank will usually need it to open a Letter of Credit or release an advance, and some importing countries ask for it with an import-permit application.</p>
<p>Two things to check line by line: the specification matches what you agreed, and the consignee details are exactly as they must appear on the shipping documents.</p>

<h2>Approving a sample and the spec sheet</h2>
<p>For a first order at meaningful volume, a sample is usually worth the time. You receive the sample together with a specification sheet that states the parameters we'll ship to. Test the sample against the sheet, not against a general impression. If the parameters are right but something isn't, such as colour, pack or label, say so now.</p>
<p>Sample availability depends on the season and the product; perishable samples need a realistic courier window so they arrive usable, and some countries require paperwork even for sample quantities. See our <a href="/sample-policy/">Sample Policy</a> for charges and handling.</p>

<h2>Decisions only the buyer can make</h2>
<p>We can recommend, but these choices sit with you, and each one affects timing:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Incoterm and named port.</strong> FOB if you have a forwarder with good rates; CIF or CFR if you'd rather we book freight. The <a href="/resources/incoterms-guide/">Incoterms Guide</a> sets out the trade-offs. The terms themselves are defined by the <a href="https://iccwbo.org/business-solutions/incoterms-rules/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">ICC's Incoterms® 2020 rules</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Payment method.</strong> An irrevocable LC at sight, an advance with balance against documents, or TT. A first order of larger value usually suits an LC; a small trial often suits TT or CAD. See <a href="/payment-terms/">Payment Terms</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Specification tolerances.</strong> Which parameters are critical to you, and what range is acceptable. Tighter tolerances can narrow availability.</li>
  <li><strong>Packaging and labelling.</strong> Bag or carton size, your brand or ours, and any language or labelling rules in your market.</li>
  <li><strong>Inspection.</strong> Our standard pre-shipment check, or a third-party agency of your choice. {{ site.inspectionStatement }}</li>
  <li><strong>Documents for your market.</strong> {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</li>
  <li><strong>Trial or full container.</strong> Our default MOQ is {{ site.defaultMoq }}.</li>
</ul>

<h2>While the goods are being readied</h2>
<p>Once payment security is in place, the goods are sourced or drawn from stock, graded and packed to the agreed specification. For fresh produce this means harvest and pre-cooling timed to the sailing; for processed goods it means a production slot against your shipment window.</p>
<p>You don't need to do much in this phase, but a few things help. Confirm the final consignee and notify-party details. If you're on FOB, put your forwarder in touch with us so their vessel booking lines up with our packing and the port cut-off. If you nominated an inspection agency, confirm they have the inspection date. And if an import permit is pending, chase it now; a container that arrives before the permit is a costly problem.</p>

<h2>The paperwork you receive, and when</h2>
<p>{{ site.complianceStatement }} Before loading, we confirm the document list against your compliance checklist so nothing is discovered missing at arrival.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Before shipment:</strong> proforma invoice, specification sheet, and the inspection report if an inspection was booked.</li>
  <li><strong>At or just after loading:</strong> commercial invoice, packing list, and a copy of the bill of lading.</li>
  <li><strong>As each is issued:</strong> certificate of origin, phytosanitary certificate, fumigation certificate and lab reports, where your product and market need them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Certificates of origin for trade agreements are issued through India's <a href="https://www.dgft.gov.in/CP/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">DGFT portal</a>, which also administers the IEC that every Indian exporter must hold. For agricultural and processed food products, <a href="https://apeda.gov.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">APEDA</a> is the export development authority; some products, basmati rice for example, carry an APEDA contract registration before loading.</p>

<h2>Bill of lading to arrival at your port</h2>
<p>The shipping line issues the bill of lading once the container is on board. It's the document of title for sea cargo, so its consignee and notify-party details matter: they decide who can take delivery at your end. We send you a copy as soon as it's issued, with the vessel name and expected arrival date.</p>
<p>How the originals reach you depends on the payment method. Under an LC or Cash Against Documents they travel through the banks, and you receive them once the payment conditions are met. Under TT they come to you directly once the balance is settled. Either way, we aim to get documents to you before the vessel arrives, so your customs broker isn't waiting on paperwork.</p>
<p>From arrival, import clearance, duties and delivery to your warehouse are yours or your broker's, under all four Incoterms we quote.</p>

<h2>First-order pitfalls we see most often</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A vague specification.</strong> It causes more back-and-forth than any other issue, and it's the source of most disputes that happen later.</li>
  <li><strong>Letting the price offer expire.</strong> Internal approvals take longer than expected, the window passes and the price has to be refreshed.</li>
  <li><strong>Payment security arriving late.</strong> Goods are readied against your shipment window, but work starts when the LC or advance is in place. LC opening time depends on your bank, so start that process as soon as the proforma is agreed.</li>
  <li><strong>LC terms that don't match the proforma.</strong> A documentary requirement or date in the LC that differs from what was agreed means an amendment, which costs time. Send us the draft LC before your bank issues it.</li>
  <li><strong>Documents requested after loading.</strong> Some certificates must be arranged before the goods leave. Share your compliance checklist at the start.</li>
  <li><strong>Consignee details changed late.</strong> Correcting a bill of lading after issue is slower than getting it right on the proforma.</li>
  <li><strong>Choosing FOB without a forwarder.</strong> Booking freight as a first-time, one-off customer can cost more than the exporter's arranged freight.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why the second order is quicker</h2>
<p>Once specification, payment terms and document requirements are agreed, a repeat order is mostly confirmation: the spec sheet, proforma format, document list and consignee details carry over. That's why we'd rather start with a trial order we can deliver well than oversell a first shipment. When you're ready, send your requirement through the <a href="/get-a-quote/">quote form</a> and we'll come back within 24 business hours.</p>
